window.COZY_SAMPLE = {"seed":"sample-crumb-of-suspicion","setup":{"penName":"Sample Author","theme":"Bakery","chapters":50,"wordsPerPuzzle":12,"gridSize":13,"difficulty":"medium","year":2026},"planText":"{\n  \"titles\": [\"A Crumb of Suspicion\", \"Murder at the Harvest Bake-Off\", \"The Foxglove Tea Affair\"],\n  \"subtitle\": \"A Cozy Mystery Word Search Book: 50 Chapters, 50 Puzzles and One Killer to Unmask\",\n  \"blurb\": \"The Pennywhistle Harbor Harvest Bake-Off was supposed to be the sweetest day of the year. Then the famous food critic Lionel Crane took one bite of plum cake and collapsed at the judging table. Hattie Bramble, retired schoolteacher and owner of the Rolling Pin Bakery, knows her friend Delia never poisoned anyone. With her tabby cat Biscuit at her heels and her nephew on the local police force, Hattie starts asking questions. Read a short chapter, then solve the word search built from it. But watch closely: in 21 of the puzzles, one word on the list is missing from the grid. Each missing word is a clue. Collect all 21 in your Detective's Notebook, solve the Final Reveal, and the leftover letters will spell out the name of the killer. Fifty chapters, fifty-one puzzles, large clear print and full solutions.\",\n  \"keywords\": [\"cozy mystery word search\", \"word search for adults\", \"mystery puzzle book\", \"large print word search\", \"whodunit puzzle book\", \"bakery cozy mystery\", \"gift for mystery lovers\"],\n  \"setting\": \"Pennywhistle Harbor, a small fishing village on the coast of Maine, during the week of the autumn Harvest Bake-Off. Key places: the Rolling Pin Bakery on the village square, the Gull's Rest Inn and its walled garden and potting shed, the striped tea tent on the green, Winnie's bookshop, the harbor, and the lighthouse.\",\n  \"sleuth\": { \"name\": \"Hattie Bramble\", \"age\": 63, \"job\": \"retired schoolteacher who now runs the Rolling Pin Bakery\", \"trait\": \"never forgets a face or a recipe, and writes everything down in a little green notebook\" },\n  \"victim\": { \"name\": \"Lionel Crane\", \"who\": \"a famous and sharp-tongued television food critic and cookbook author, invited as head judge of the Harvest Bake-Off\" },\n  \"suspects\": [\n    { \"name\": \"Delia Fairweather\", \"role\": \"owner of the Sugar and Spoon bakery across the square\", \"motive\": \"Lionel's cruel review closed her first cafe in Boston years ago\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Rupert Ashby\", \"role\": \"owner of the Gull's Rest Inn, where Lionel was staying\", \"motive\": \"deep in debt, and Lionel had offered to buy the inn for a fraction of its worth\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Colette Marsh\", \"role\": \"Lionel's quiet personal assistant for twelve years\", \"motive\": \"Lionel built his famous cookbook on her recipes and planned to fire her and publish her new book under his own name\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Gordon Pike\", \"role\": \"harbor master and chairman of the bake-off committee\", \"motive\": \"Lionel knew Gordon had rigged old bake-off results and was pressing him for favors\" },\n    { \"name\": \"Imogen Vale\", \"role\": \"Lionel's estranged niece, a painter who lives by the lighthouse\", \"motive\": \"she stands to inherit Lionel's house and savings\" }\n  ],\n  \"killer\": \"Colette Marsh\",\n  \"solution\": \"Colette Marsh poisoned Lionel because he stole her recipes and was about to fire her and take her new book. She steeped foxglove leaves from the inn garden in his morning tea, sweetened it with honey to hide the bitter taste, and forged an order so that Delia's plum cake would be blamed when he collapsed at the tasting.\",\n  \"outline\": [\n    { \"n\": 1, \"title\": \"The Week of the Bake-Off\", \"beat\": \"Hattie bakes at dawn while the village buzzes about the famous critic Lionel Crane coming to judge the Harvest Bake-Off.\" },\n    { \"n\": 2, \"title\": \"A Very Particular Guest\", \"beat\": \"Lionel arrives at the Gull's Rest Inn, insults Hattie's scones, and his assistant Colette carefully unpacks his monogrammed teacup.\" },\n    { \"n\": 3, \"title\": \"Dinner with Daggers Drawn\", \"beat\": \"At the welcome dinner Hattie notices that Delia, Rupert, Gordon and Imogen each bristle at Lionel.\" },\n    { \"n\": 4, \"title\": \"Steam in the Blue Suite\", \"beat\": \"Delivering pastries to the inn at dawn, Hattie glimpses a travel kettle steaming in Lionel's suite.\" },\n    { \"n\": 5, \"title\": \"Trouble in the Tea Tent\", \"beat\": \"During judging Lionel tastes Delia's plum cake, then collapses at the table and is rushed away.\" },\n    { \"n\": 6, \"title\": \"Grim News on the Green\", \"beat\": \"Deputy Sam Bramble tells his aunt that Lionel has died and the doctor suspects poison; Delia's cake is taken away.\" },\n    { \"n\": 7, \"title\": \"Something in the Garden\", \"beat\": \"Walking Biscuit past the inn's walled garden, Hattie spots freshly cut foxglove stalks.\" },\n    { \"n\": 8, \"title\": \"Whispers About Delia\", \"beat\": \"Gossip turns against Delia, and Hattie decides she cannot sit by while her friend is blamed.\" },\n    { \"n\": 9, \"title\": \"The Empty Hook\", \"beat\": \"In the inn's potting shed Hattie finds an empty hook where the garden shears should hang.\" },\n    { \"n\": 10, \"title\": \"Cocoa and a Suspect List\", \"beat\": \"Hattie and Winnie from the bookshop draw up a list of suspects over cocoa after closing time.\" },\n    { \"n\": 11, \"title\": \"Laundry Day at the Inn\", \"beat\": \"Helping the inn's housekeeper, Hattie finds an apron with odd green stains in the laundry basket.\" },\n    { \"n\": 12, \"title\": \"An Innkeeper's Worries\", \"beat\": \"Hattie learns that Rupert is deep in debt and that Lionel had offered to buy the inn cheaply.\" },\n    { \"n\": 13, \"title\": \"Old Scorecards\", \"beat\": \"At the harbor Gordon Pike acts nervous when Hattie mentions the bake-off scorecards from years past.\" },\n    { \"n\": 14, \"title\": \"A Taste He Hated\", \"beat\": \"Sam lets slip that Lionel's teacup held traces of honey, though Lionel was famous for hating honey.\" },\n    { \"n\": 15, \"title\": \"The Painter by the Light\", \"beat\": \"Imogen Vale visits the bakery, admits she inherits Lionel's house, and says she was painting at the lighthouse.\" },\n    { \"n\": 16, \"title\": \"Lost and Found\", \"beat\": \"In the inn's lost-and-found box Hattie finds a train ticket stub dated a day before the bake-off guests arrived.\" },\n    { \"n\": 17, \"title\": \"Twelve Years of Tea\", \"beat\": \"Colette tells Hattie how devoted she was to Lionel and says she spent the bake-off morning at the library.\" },\n    { \"n\": 18, \"title\": \"Recipes in the Margins\", \"beat\": \"A guest leaves a handwritten recipe notebook at the bakery, and its recipes match Lionel's famous cookbook.\" },\n    { \"n\": 19, \"title\": \"A Nasty Note\", \"beat\": \"Someone tapes a cruel note to Delia's shop window, and Hattie helps her clean it away.\" },\n    { \"n\": 20, \"title\": \"Biscuit Knocks a Jar\", \"beat\": \"While Hattie bakes, Biscuit knocks over a spice jar and gives her an idea about timing.\" },\n    { \"n\": 21, \"title\": \"A Package for a Dead Man\", \"beat\": \"A courier delivers a parcel for Lionel to Winnie's bookshop: a publishing contract for a new cookbook in his name alone.\" },\n    { \"n\": 22, \"title\": \"The Harbor Master Confesses\", \"beat\": \"Gordon admits he once rigged the bake-off for his late sister and that Lionel was pressuring him about it.\" },\n    { \"n\": 23, \"title\": \"Words Never Sent\", \"beat\": \"In the inn's writing desk Hattie finds a draft letter from Lionel firing someone close to him.\" },\n    { \"n\": 24, \"title\": \"Stay Out of It, Aunt Hattie\", \"beat\": \"Sam warns Hattie to stop poking around because the county detective is focused on Delia.\" },\n    { \"n\": 25, \"title\": \"The Innkeeper's Fib\", \"beat\": \"Hattie catches Rupert in a lie about where he was on the morning of the bake-off.\" },\n    { \"n\": 26, \"title\": \"A Scent in the Shed\", \"beat\": \"Back in the potting shed Hattie catches the faint smell of lavender hand cream.\" },\n    { \"n\": 27, \"title\": \"A Fisherman's Word\", \"beat\": \"A fisherman confirms he saw Imogen at the lighthouse early that morning, though not the whole time.\" },\n    { \"n\": 28, \"title\": \"Biscuit Digs In\", \"beat\": \"Biscuit digs under a hydrangea in the inn garden and uncovers a muddy trowel.\" },\n    { \"n\": 29, \"title\": \"The Village Rallies\", \"beat\": \"Delia is named a person of interest, and Hattie rallies the village to buy from her shop.\" },\n    { \"n\": 30, \"title\": \"Small Muddy Boots\", \"beat\": \"Hattie notices a pair of small muddy boots drying on the inn's back porch.\" },\n    { \"n\": 31, \"title\": \"Rupert Comes Clean\", \"beat\": \"Rupert confesses he was meeting a bank officer in secret, not poisoning anyone.\" },\n    { \"n\": 32, \"title\": \"Candlelight and Clues\", \"beat\": \"A storm knocks out the power, and Hattie and Winnie review every clue by candlelight.\" },\n    { \"n\": 33, \"title\": \"Leaves in a Guidebook\", \"beat\": \"Tucked inside a guidebook in the inn's reading room, Hattie finds an envelope of crushed dried leaves.\" },\n    { \"n\": 34, \"title\": \"Clear Skies for Gordon\", \"beat\": \"Radio logs prove Gordon was at the harbor office all morning, so Hattie crosses him off.\" },\n    { \"n\": 35, \"title\": \"Left Behind in the Tent\", \"beat\": \"Hattie rescues a saucer from the tea tent's leftover crockery, and Sam sends it to the lab.\" },\n    { \"n\": 36, \"title\": \"Stick to Pies\", \"beat\": \"A note slipped under the bakery door warns Hattie to stick to pies.\" },\n    { \"n\": 37, \"title\": \"Who Knew the Schedule\", \"beat\": \"The judging timetable shows that only the judges' staff knew when the plum cake would be tasted.\" },\n    { \"n\": 38, \"title\": \"The Will That Wasn't\", \"beat\": \"Imogen reveals Lionel had never changed his will, which clears away her motive.\" },\n    { \"n\": 39, \"title\": \"Shelves of Seed Catalogs\", \"beat\": \"Hattie spends an afternoon in the inn's reading room among old gardening books and seed catalogs.\" },\n    { \"n\": 40, \"title\": \"A Pressed Leaf\", \"beat\": \"In a gardening book Hattie finds a pressed foxglove leaf used as a bookmark.\" },\n    { \"n\": 41, \"title\": \"Not Enough for Sam\", \"beat\": \"Hattie shares her worries with Sam, who says he needs real proof.\" },\n    { \"n\": 42, \"title\": \"A Hand That Wasn't Delia's\", \"beat\": \"The order form for Delia's plum cake carries a forged signature that is not Delia's.\" },\n    { \"n\": 43, \"title\": \"The Cake Was Clean\", \"beat\": \"Lab results show the saucer held foxglove but the cake was clean, and Delia is cleared.\" },\n    { \"n\": 44, \"title\": \"Closed on Tuesdays\", \"beat\": \"The librarian says the library was closed that morning, so one suspect's alibi falls apart.\" },\n    { \"n\": 45, \"title\": \"An Invitation to Tea\", \"beat\": \"Hattie invites all five suspects to a thank-you tea at the Rolling Pin.\" },\n    { \"n\": 46, \"title\": \"Honey and Lavender\", \"beat\": \"At the tea Hattie chats about honey and lavender and watches one cup rattle on its saucer.\" },\n    { \"n\": 47, \"title\": \"The Photograph\", \"beat\": \"Winnie shows Hattie a snapshot from bake-off morning with a figure slipping out of the inn garden at dawn.\" },\n    { \"n\": 48, \"title\": \"The Green Notebook\", \"beat\": \"Late at night Hattie lays out every clue in her notebook and the pieces fall together.\" },\n    { \"n\": 49, \"title\": \"The Same Hand\", \"beat\": \"Hattie compares the writing on an old recipe card with the forged signature, and they match.\" },\n    { \"n\": 50, \"title\": \"Sunset in the Garden\", \"beat\": \"Hattie gathers everyone in the inn garden at sunset and raises her hand to name the killer.\" }\n  ],\n  \"clues\": [\n    { \"chapter\": 2, \"word\": \"TEACUP\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 4, \"word\": \"KETTLE\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 7, \"word\": \"FOXGLOVE\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 9, \"word\": \"SHEARS\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 11, \"word\": \"APRON\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 14, \"word\": \"HONEY\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 16, \"word\": \"TICKET\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 18, \"word\": \"NOTEBOOK\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 21, \"word\": \"CONTRACT\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 23, \"word\": \"LETTER\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 26, \"word\": \"LAVENDER\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 28, \"word\": \"TROWEL\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 30, \"word\": \"BOOTS\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 33, \"word\": \"ENVELOPE\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 35, \"word\": \"SAUCER\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 37, \"word\": \"TIMETABLE\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 40, \"word\": \"BOOKMARK\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 42, \"word\": \"SIGNATURE\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 44, \"word\": \"ALIBI\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 47, \"word\": \"SNAPSHOT\" },\n    { \"chapter\": 49, \"word\": \"RECIPE\" }\n  ]\n}\n","batchText":["{\r\n \"chapters\": [\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 1,\r\n   \"title\": \"The Week of the Bake-Off\",\r\n   \"text\": \"The ovens at the Rolling Pin Bakery were warm long before the sun climbed over Pennywhistle Harbor. Hattie Bramble tied back her silver hair, switched on the radio, and began rolling out cinnamon dough while the gulls complained outside. Biscuit, her orange tabby, supervised from the windowsill with the serious air of a cat who expected a reward for his trouble.\\n\\nBy seven o'clock the little bell over the door had not stopped ringing, and everyone seemed to carry the same news. Lionel Crane, the famous television critic, was coming to judge the Harvest Bake-Off. Abe Tolliver, still smelling of salt and bait, bought two blueberry muffins and announced that Crane had once made a grown chef cry on national television. \\\"Gave a pie one star because the crust was shy,\\\" Abe said darkly.\\n\\nHattie laughed and wrapped his muffins in waxed paper, but she felt a small flutter of nerves all the same. Across the square, the windows of the Sugar and Spoon bakery glowed gold. Her friend Delia Fairweather was surely up early too, fussing over the plum cake she planned to enter.\\n\\nWhen the morning rush finally eased, Hattie took out her little green notebook and wrote the date at the top of a fresh page. She had kept notes on recipes, customers, and village doings for years, and something told her this week would need plenty of pages. Biscuit yawned, thoroughly unimpressed, and went back to sleep in the sun.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"Hattie\",\r\n    \"Biscuit\",\r\n    \"ovens\",\r\n    \"cinnamon\",\r\n    \"Lionel\",\r\n    \"critic\",\r\n    \"muffins\",\r\n    \"Tolliver\",\r\n    \"Delia\",\r\n    \"plum\",\r\n    \"harbor\",\r\n    \"windowsill\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 2,\r\n   \"title\": \"A Very Particular Guest\",\r\n   \"text\": \"Lionel Crane arrived at the Gull's Rest Inn the next afternoon in a long silver car that looked far too grand for the gravel drive. Hattie happened to be standing in the front hall with a basket of welcome scones, because Rupert Ashby, the innkeeper, had ordered two dozen to make a good impression.\\n\\nThe critic was tall and thin, with a silk scarf and eyebrows that seemed permanently raised. He lifted a scone, sniffed it, and set it down again. \\\"Dry,\\\" he declared. \\\"Dry as a Sunday sermon, and far too much orange zest.\\\" Hattie's cheeks went warm, but she only smiled. Thirty years of teaching seventh grade had given her a very thick skin.\\n\\nBehind Lionel came a small, quiet woman carrying three suitcases and a padded case. This was Colette Marsh, who had been his assistant for twelve years. While Rupert fussed over the luggage, Colette knelt and opened the padded case with great care. Nested in tissue paper lay a fine white teacup painted with a gold monogram. \\\"Mr. Crane drinks only from his own,\\\" Colette explained softly. \\\"And never with honey. He can't abide it.\\\" \\\"Quite right,\\\" Lionel sniffed. \\\"Hotel china is a crime.\\\"\\n\\nOn the walk home, Hattie took out her green notebook and wrote down the teacup, the scarf, and the insult to her scones, in that order. Then, for good measure, she underlined the insult twice.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"teacup\",\r\n    \"Lionel\",\r\n    \"scones\",\r\n    \"Rupert\",\r\n    \"Colette\",\r\n    \"scarf\",\r\n    \"eyebrows\",\r\n    \"sermon\",\r\n    \"suitcases\",\r\n    \"monogram\",\r\n    \"tissue\",\r\n    \"luggage\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 3,\r\n   \"title\": \"Dinner with Daggers Drawn\",\r\n   \"text\": \"The welcome dinner was held in the long dining room of the Gull's Rest Inn, under a chandelier that tinkled whenever the wind gusted off the bay. Hattie sat beside Winnie Calloway, with a fine view of everyone at the table. Lionel Crane held court at the head of it. He sent back the chowder for being lukewarm, then praised the bread so faintly that it sounded like an insult.\\n\\nWhen Delia Fairweather passed him the butter, he peered at her. \\\"Boston, wasn't it? That sad little cafe with the soggy tarts?\\\" Delia's fork clattered onto her plate. Hattie remembered, with a pang, that one cruel review from Lionel had closed Delia's first cafe years ago.\\n\\nRupert Ashby fared no better. Lionel remarked on the peeling paint and the creaky stairs, and Rupert's knuckles went white around his wineglass. Gordon Pike, the harbor master and chairman of the bake-off committee, laughed too loudly at every joke until Lionel murmured, \\\"We must talk about the old scores, Gordon.\\\" After that, Gordon stopped laughing altogether. At the far end sat Imogen Vale, Lionel's niece, with paint on her cuffs. She never once called him uncle, and she left before dessert.\\n\\nOnly Colette Marsh seemed calm, quietly refilling Lionel's water glass. \\\"Well,\\\" Winnie whispered over her pudding, \\\"if looks could do harm, that man would need a bodyguard.\\\" Hattie said nothing, but beneath the tablecloth she was already writing in her notebook.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"chandelier\",\r\n    \"Winnie\",\r\n    \"chowder\",\r\n    \"Delia\",\r\n    \"Boston\",\r\n    \"tarts\",\r\n    \"Rupert\",\r\n    \"wineglass\",\r\n    \"Gordon\",\r\n    \"Imogen\",\r\n    \"pudding\",\r\n    \"tablecloth\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 4,\r\n   \"title\": \"Steam in the Blue Suite\",\r\n   \"text\": \"On the morning of the bake-off, Hattie was up even earlier than usual. Rupert had ordered a tray of almond croissants for his guests, and the sky over the harbor was still pale pink when she carried the box through the kitchen door of the Gull's Rest Inn. Dottie Quill, the housekeeper, was already at the stove. \\\"Bless you, Hattie,\\\" she said. \\\"Mr. Crane wants his breakfast brought up to the Blue Suite, and if I climb those stairs once more my knees will file a complaint.\\\"\\n\\nSo Hattie climbed them herself, balancing the tray. The door of the Blue Suite stood open a few inches. Through the gap she glimpsed a little travel kettle on the dresser, steam curling from its spout, and beside it the monogrammed cup she had seen unpacked. The air smelled of tea, but with a sharp, green edge to it, like a meadow after rain.\\n\\n\\\"Leave it on the table outside,\\\" called a voice from deeper in the room. Hattie could not quite tell whose it was. She set down the tray and tiptoed away, feeling oddly like a schoolgirl caught passing notes. Downstairs, Dottie poured her a cup of coffee and chattered about the judging.\\n\\nOn the walk back to the Rolling Pin, Hattie paused by a lamppost and wrote in her green notebook: kettle, steam, green smell. She could not have said why. It simply seemed worth remembering.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"kettle\",\r\n    \"almond\",\r\n    \"croissants\",\r\n    \"Dottie\",\r\n    \"stairs\",\r\n    \"dresser\",\r\n    \"steam\",\r\n    \"spout\",\r\n    \"meadow\",\r\n    \"schoolgirl\",\r\n    \"coffee\",\r\n    \"lamppost\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 5,\r\n   \"title\": \"Trouble in the Tea Tent\",\r\n   \"text\": \"By ten o'clock the striped tea tent on the village green was packed to its canvas walls. Long tables groaned under pies, tarts, loaves, and layer cakes, each with a numbered card. Hattie had entered her maple pecan pie, mostly to keep Winnie company, though Winnie had entered nothing and appointed herself chief taster of crumbs.\\n\\nGordon Pike rang a little brass bell, and the judging began. Lionel Crane moved down the table with Colette at his elbow, holding his notes. He looked pale, Hattie thought, and he squinted at the bunting as though the light bothered him. \\\"Too sweet,\\\" he said of a lemon bar. \\\"Tragic,\\\" he said of a rhubarb crumble. He tasted Hattie's pie and pronounced it \\\"adequate,\\\" which from Lionel was practically a hug.\\n\\nThen he reached Delia's plum cake, and the whole tent seemed to hold its breath. Lionel took one forkful and chewed slowly. He opened his mouth to speak, but no words came. His hand fluttered to his chest, the fork slipped from his fingers, and he sank sideways against the table, scattering programs onto the grass.\\n\\nRupert Ashby shouted for a doctor. Within minutes an ambulance bumped across the green, lights flashing, and Lionel was carried away on a stretcher. In the hush that followed, Hattie stood very still, her heart thumping. Beside the overturned chair lay the half-eaten slice of plum cake, glistening and dark.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"canvas\",\r\n    \"maple\",\r\n    \"pecan\",\r\n    \"Gordon\",\r\n    \"bunting\",\r\n    \"rhubarb\",\r\n    \"crumble\",\r\n    \"Delia\",\r\n    \"forkful\",\r\n    \"ambulance\",\r\n    \"stretcher\",\r\n    \"programs\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 6,\r\n   \"title\": \"Grim News on the Green\",\r\n   \"text\": \"The tea tent emptied slowly, like a teapot draining. Hattie stayed behind to gather the scattered programs, and Biscuit, who had followed her from the bakery, sat beneath a table with his tail wrapped tight around his paws.\\n\\nLate in the afternoon, Deputy Sam Bramble came striding across the green. Hattie had watched her nephew grow from a freckled boy who stole cookie dough into a tall young man with a badge, but she had never seen him look so solemn. \\\"Aunt Hattie,\\\" he said quietly, taking off his hat. \\\"Mr. Crane died at the county hospital an hour ago. The doctor thinks it might have been poison.\\\" Hattie sat down hard on a folding chair. \\\"Poison? At the bake-off?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I shouldn't tell you anything,\\\" Sam said, which meant he was about to tell her more. \\\"The county is sending a detective. Until she gets here, nobody touches the food.\\\" The detective arrived soon after, a brisk woman in a gray raincoat who introduced herself as Lorna Hask. She glanced over the tables, asked three sharp questions, and pointed straight at the plum cake. \\\"That one,\\\" she said. \\\"Bag it.\\\"\\n\\nAcross the tent, Delia Fairweather pressed a hand to her mouth as Sam lifted the cake into an evidence box. Hattie scooped up Biscuit and held him close. She did not believe for a moment that Delia had harmed anyone. But she could see exactly how it looked.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"teapot\",\r\n    \"Biscuit\",\r\n    \"nephew\",\r\n    \"freckled\",\r\n    \"cookie\",\r\n    \"badge\",\r\n    \"hospital\",\r\n    \"doctor\",\r\n    \"poison\",\r\n    \"raincoat\",\r\n    \"Lorna\",\r\n    \"evidence\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 7,\r\n   \"title\": \"Something in the Garden\",\r\n   \"text\": \"The next morning was gray and still, and the whole village seemed to be holding its breath. Hattie closed the bakery for an hour and took Biscuit for his walk. Biscuit did not believe in leashes, but he trotted at her heels like a small orange dog, stopping now and then to glare at a sparrow.\\n\\nTheir path led past the Gull's Rest Inn and its old walled garden. The brick walls were thick with ivy, and the wooden gate hung slightly open. Biscuit slipped through before Hattie could stop him. \\\"Come back here, you rascal,\\\" she whispered, and followed. The garden was lovely even in October, full of asters, late roses, and seed heads rattling in the breeze.\\n\\nAlong the far wall stood a row of tall foxglove, a few purple bells still clinging to the stems. But several stalks had been cut off near the ground. The ends were clean and pale, the sap still damp to the touch. Hattie frowned. Her grandmother had grown foxglove and warned her never to let the leaves near the kitchen. The plant was beautiful, but it could stop a heart.\\n\\nShe glanced up at the windows of the inn, half expecting a face to appear. None did. Biscuit sniffed the cut stalks, sneezed, and looked up at her as if to ask what she meant to do about it. \\\"I'm going to write it down,\\\" Hattie told him, reaching for her green notebook. \\\"That's what I'm going to do.\\\"\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"foxglove\",\r\n    \"Biscuit\",\r\n    \"leashes\",\r\n    \"sparrow\",\r\n    \"walled\",\r\n    \"rascal\",\r\n    \"asters\",\r\n    \"roses\",\r\n    \"purple\",\r\n    \"stalks\",\r\n    \"sneezed\",\r\n    \"October\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 8,\r\n   \"title\": \"Whispers About Delia\",\r\n   \"text\": \"By lunchtime the whole of Pennywhistle Harbor seemed to have decided what had happened, and none of it was kind. Hattie heard it in the bakery line, between orders of rye bread and pumpkin muffins. \\\"Everyone knows she hated him,\\\" said a woman from the church choir, lowering her voice so that only half the shop could hear. \\\"He ruined her in Boston.\\\" \\\"And it was her cake he ate,\\\" added the postman, accepting his change with a meaningful look.\\n\\nHattie set down the tongs harder than she meant to. \\\"Delia Fairweather has fed half this village through two blizzards and a power outage,\\\" she said. \\\"I won't hear another word.\\\"\\n\\nThe bell jingled, and Winnie Calloway hurried in from the bookshop next door, her scarf askew and her eyes bright with news. \\\"Detective Hask questioned Delia for two whole hours,\\\" she whispered across the counter. \\\"And the Sugar and Spoon has a sign saying closed until further notice. Poor lamb.\\\" Hattie looked out across the square. Delia's shop stood dark and silent, its pretty blue awning sagging in the damp. She pictured her friend alone inside among the mixing bowls, listening to the town turn against her.\\n\\nBiscuit wound around her ankles and gave a firm meow, as if he had reached the same conclusion. \\\"You're right,\\\" Hattie said, brushing the flour from her hands. \\\"Winnie, mind the counter for ten minutes. I'm taking Delia a pie, and then I'm going to find out what really happened.\\\"\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"lunchtime\",\r\n    \"pumpkin\",\r\n    \"Boston\",\r\n    \"postman\",\r\n    \"tongs\",\r\n    \"blizzards\",\r\n    \"Winnie\",\r\n    \"bookshop\",\r\n    \"Hask\",\r\n    \"awning\",\r\n    \"mixing\",\r\n    \"ankles\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 9,\r\n   \"title\": \"The Empty Hook\",\r\n   \"text\": \"That afternoon Hattie returned to the Gull's Rest Inn with a tin of ginger cookies for Dottie Quill, who had looked so frazzled at the bake-off. She found the housekeeper in the back garden, pinning sheets to the line. \\\"You're an angel,\\\" Dottie said, taking a cookie. \\\"Mr. Ashby wants twine from the potting shed, but my hands are full. Would you mind?\\\"\\n\\nThe potting shed stood in a corner of the walled garden, a crooked little building with a mossy roof. Inside it smelled of damp soil and old clay pots. A pegboard covered one whole wall, and someone very tidy had painted the outline of each tool in white: rake, hoe, spade, hand fork, and a long pruning saw. Every tool hung neatly inside its outline but one. Halfway up the board, a sturdy brass hook stood empty, and the painted shape around it was unmistakable. It was the outline of a pair of garden shears.\\n\\nHattie found the twine and carried it back out. \\\"Dottie,\\\" she asked lightly, \\\"has anyone borrowed the garden shears lately?\\\" Dottie frowned. \\\"Not that I know of. Mr. Ashby is fussy about that pegboard. He'd have a fit if anything went missing.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"I expect they'll turn up,\\\" Hattie said. But walking home, she thought of the cut foxglove stalks, so clean and straight. She opened her green notebook, wrote one word, and underlined it twice.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"shears\",\r\n    \"ginger\",\r\n    \"Dottie\",\r\n    \"sheets\",\r\n    \"twine\",\r\n    \"potting\",\r\n    \"mossy\",\r\n    \"pegboard\",\r\n    \"pruning\",\r\n    \"brass\",\r\n    \"outline\",\r\n    \"spade\"\r\n   ]\r\n  },\r\n  {\r\n   \"n\": 10,\r\n   \"title\": \"Cocoa and a Suspect List\",\r\n   \"text\": \"After closing time, Hattie locked the Rolling Pin and slipped next door to Winnie's bookshop. The shop was dim and cozy, lit only by a green lamp on the counter. Winnie had two mugs of cocoa waiting, each crowned with a lopsided marshmallow, and Biscuit had already claimed the armchair by the radiator. \\\"Right,\\\" said Winnie. \\\"Who would want that dreadful man gone?\\\"\\n\\nHattie opened her green notebook to a clean page. \\\"We go by what we saw,\\\" she said in her old classroom voice. \\\"No guessing.\\\" \\\"Rupert Ashby, then,\\\" said Winnie. \\\"Lionel insulted his inn to his face.\\\" Hattie wrote it down. \\\"Gordon Pike. Lionel wanted to talk about the old scores, and Gordon went white as flour.\\\" \\\"Imogen Vale,\\\" Winnie added, licking cocoa from her lip. \\\"His own niece, and she wouldn't even say goodnight. Family quarrels run deepest.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"And Colette Marsh,\\\" Hattie said slowly. \\\"She knew his habits better than anyone. His teacup, his schedule, everything.\\\" Winnie tapped the page. \\\"You have to put Delia down too, you know. Fair's fair.\\\"\\n\\nHattie sighed and wrote the name, though her pen pressed hard enough to dent the paper beneath. Five names now sat in a neat column, and below them, the shears and the foxglove. Outside, the harbor bell clanged softly. Biscuit stretched, yawned, and curled into a tighter ball, as if to say the case would keep until morning.\",\r\n   \"words\": [\r\n    \"bookshop\",\r\n    \"cocoa\",\r\n    \"armchair\",\r\n    \"radiator\",\r\n    \"Rupert\",\r\n    \"classroom\",\r\n    \"Gordon\",\r\n    \"flour\",\r\n    \"Imogen\",\r\n    \"Colette\",\r\n    \"quarrels\",\r\n    \"column\"\r\n   ]\r\n  }\r\n ]\r\n}","{\n  \"chapters\": [\n    {\n      \"n\": 11,\n      \"title\": \"Laundry Day at the Inn\",\n      \"text\": \"Monday morning brought gray skies and a mountain of washing to the Gull's Rest Inn. Dottie Quill met Hattie at the back door with a sigh as big as the harbor. \\\"Half the guests packed up and left after poor Mr. Crane,\\\" she said, \\\"and every one of them left their sheets behind. I could use an extra pair of hands.\\\" Hattie was glad to help, though she also hoped to look around a little.\\n\\nShe rolled up her sleeves and followed Dottie into the steamy laundry room, where a big wicker basket sat overflowing beside the machines. Biscuit, who had trotted along uninvited, curled up on a stack of folded towels as if he owned them. The two women sorted pillowcases from tablecloths and chatted about the weather.\\n\\nNear the bottom of the basket, Hattie's fingers closed on a crumpled kitchen apron. She shook it out. Across the front ran odd green stains, darker than grass and badly smeared, as though someone had wiped their hands in a hurry. \\\"Whose is this?\\\" she asked.\\n\\nDottie squinted at it. \\\"Could be anyone's. Rupert keeps a stack by the kitchen for guests who like to help with breakfast. Folks borrow them and toss them back.\\\" She reached for the starch. \\\"Why, does it matter?\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Probably not,\\\" said Hattie lightly. But when Dottie turned away, she slipped out her little green notebook and wrote down the date, the color of the stains, and the faint leafy smell that clung to the cotton.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Hattie\",\n        \"Dottie\",\n        \"apron\",\n        \"laundry\",\n        \"basket\",\n        \"stains\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"towels\",\n        \"kitchen\",\n        \"starch\",\n        \"Rupert\",\n        \"cotton\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 12,\n      \"title\": \"An Innkeeper's Worries\",\n      \"text\": \"On Tuesday Hattie carried a basket of blueberry muffins up the inn's front steps, telling herself it was simply the neighborly thing to do. Rupert Ashby sat in his cramped little office behind the front desk, his reading glasses pushed up into his silver hair and a mug of coffee gone cold at his elbow.\\n\\nThe desk was buried in envelopes. Several wore bright red stamps that said PAST DUE, and one from the bank had been opened, read, and crumpled. Rupert swept them into a drawer when he saw her, but not quite fast enough. \\\"Muffins,\\\" he said, managing a smile. \\\"You are a kind woman, Hattie Bramble.\\\"\\n\\nShe sat down without being asked, a trick she had learned in thirty years of teaching. Before long the whole story tumbled out. The roof leaked, the boiler groaned, and bookings had been thin since spring. \\\"And then Lionel Crane strolls in,\\\" Rupert said bitterly, \\\"and offers to buy the place. My grandfather built this inn, and that man offered me a fraction of what it is worth. Said I would be lucky to get it.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"What did you tell him?\\\" Hattie asked gently. Rupert rubbed his eyes. \\\"I told him I would think about it. Well, that offer is gone now, isn't it?\\\" He heard his own words and winced. \\\"That came out wrong.\\\"\\n\\nHattie patted his hand and said nothing at all, but on the walk home she wrote the word debt in her notebook and underlined it twice.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Hattie\",\n        \"Rupert\",\n        \"muffins\",\n        \"office\",\n        \"glasses\",\n        \"coffee\",\n        \"stamps\",\n        \"drawer\",\n        \"boiler\",\n        \"Lionel\",\n        \"debt\",\n        \"bookings\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 13,\n      \"title\": \"Old Scorecards\",\n      \"text\": \"The harbor smelled of salt, diesel, and frying clams when Hattie walked down to the docks on Wednesday afternoon. Gulls wheeled over the lobster boats, and old Abe Tolliver sat mending a net on an upturned crate. He tipped his cap. \\\"Gordon's in the office,\\\" he said, \\\"grumpy as a wet cat.\\\"\\n\\nGordon Pike was indeed in the harbor office, surrounded by tide charts and a radio that crackled with boat chatter. As chairman of the bake-off committee, he had been run ragged all week, and his ruddy face looked tired. Hattie set a paper bag of ginger cookies on his desk and told him she was putting together a little scrapbook about the history of the Harvest Bake-Off.\\n\\n\\\"That's nice,\\\" Gordon said, reaching for a cookie. \\\"I thought I might borrow the old scorecards,\\\" Hattie went on. \\\"The ones from years past, with all the judges' notes. It would be such fun to see who won what.\\\" Gordon's hand stopped halfway to his mouth. The cookie snapped in two, and crumbs scattered over a tide chart.\\n\\n\\\"Those are long gone,\\\" he said quickly. \\\"Thrown out. Water damage. Ages ago.\\\" He brushed at the crumbs far longer than they needed. \\\"Nobody cares about old scores, Hattie. Best leave them be.\\\"\\n\\nHattie thanked him and wandered back up the hill, thinking hard. In her experience, people rarely got that flustered over paperwork they truly did not care about.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Hattie\",\n        \"harbor\",\n        \"Gordon\",\n        \"scorecards\",\n        \"Tolliver\",\n        \"lobster\",\n        \"radio\",\n        \"cookies\",\n        \"scrapbook\",\n        \"chairman\",\n        \"crumbs\",\n        \"diesel\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 14,\n      \"title\": \"A Taste He Hated\",\n      \"text\": \"Deputy Sam Bramble came into the Rolling Pin on Thursday morning looking as though he had not slept in a week. Hattie poured him a mug of strong coffee and set a warm cinnamon roll in front of him before he could even say hello. Biscuit wound around his boots, purring hopefully.\\n\\n\\\"Detective Hask has me running in circles,\\\" Sam groaned between bites. \\\"Forms, phone calls, more forms. And the lab results are just plain strange. They tested that fancy teacup of his, the one with his initials on it. Traces of honey, they said. Honey and tea, as far as they can tell so far.\\\" He froze, his roll halfway to his mouth. \\\"Aunt Hattie, forget I said that. I am not supposed to talk about the case.\\\"\\n\\nHattie mimed zipping her lips, but her mind was already racing. She remembered an old episode of Lionel's cooking show, where he had wrinkled his famous nose at a jar of clover honey. \\\"The sticky crutch of lazy bakers,\\\" he had called it. He had refused it in his tea for twenty years and told anyone who would listen. Every food magazine in the country had printed that line.\\n\\n\\\"Sam,\\\" she said slowly, \\\"why would a man who hated honey drink it in his tea?\\\" Sam shrugged and reached for another napkin. \\\"Maybe he changed his mind.\\\"\\n\\nHattie doubted it very much. Men like Lionel Crane did not change their minds about anything, least of all their own tea.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Deputy\",\n        \"cinnamon\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"honey\",\n        \"teacup\",\n        \"initials\",\n        \"episode\",\n        \"clover\",\n        \"napkin\",\n        \"magazine\",\n        \"crutch\",\n        \"Lionel\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 15,\n      \"title\": \"The Painter by the Light\",\n      \"text\": \"On Friday afternoon the bell over the bakery door jingled, and in walked Imogen Vale with paint on her fingers and a canvas bag over one shoulder. She was tall and windblown, with a long red scarf wrapped twice around her neck. She ordered a cup of chamomile and a slice of apple pie and chose the little table by the window.\\n\\nHattie brought the pie over herself. \\\"I'm so sorry about your uncle,\\\" she said. Imogen gave a short laugh that was not really a laugh. \\\"Thank you. Though we hadn't spoken in nine years. He told me painting was a hobby for people with no talent.\\\" She stirred her tea. \\\"I suppose you've heard I get his house. And his savings. The whole village seems to know.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Villages do talk,\\\" Hattie admitted. Imogen nodded. \\\"Well, let them. I was up at the lighthouse that morning, painting the sunrise over the rocks, same as every clear day. I didn't even know he'd collapsed until supper.\\\" She pulled a small sketchbook from her bag and flipped it open to a watercolor of the lighthouse, pink and gold against the sea.\\n\\nIt was lovely, Hattie thought. It was also unsigned and undated, and anyone could paint a sunrise on any morning at all. Biscuit hopped onto the empty chair and sniffed at the paint on Imogen's sleeve.\\n\\nAfter she left, Hattie jotted down the hours Imogen claimed to have been painting. Then, after a moment, she added a small question mark.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Imogen\",\n        \"canvas\",\n        \"scarf\",\n        \"chamomile\",\n        \"uncle\",\n        \"savings\",\n        \"lighthouse\",\n        \"sunrise\",\n        \"sketchbook\",\n        \"watercolor\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"supper\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 16,\n      \"title\": \"Lost and Found\",\n      \"text\": \"Dottie Quill kept the lost-and-found box in a cupboard under the front stairs, and on Saturday morning she hauled it out with a grunt. \\\"Detective Hask wants it all logged,\\\" she told Hattie, \\\"and my eyes aren't what they were. Would you mind reading things off while I write?\\\"\\n\\nHattie did not mind one bit. The box held the usual odds and ends of a seaside inn. There was a single leather glove, a pair of sunglasses, a child's plastic shovel, three umbrellas, a paperback with a cracked spine, and a hairbrush nobody would ever claim. Biscuit climbed straight into the box and sat on the umbrellas, purring like a little engine.\\n\\nTucked inside the paperback, marking a page, was a torn train ticket stub. Hattie held it up to the window. Boston to Portland, it read, one adult, and it was stamped with a date she knew at once. It was the day before the bake-off guests were due to arrive. Every visitor for the contest, Lionel's party included, had checked in together on the same afternoon. Hattie remembered it clearly, because she had delivered the welcome scones.\\n\\n\\\"Someone got here early,\\\" she murmured. Dottie peered over her glasses. \\\"Or someone came and went. Folks drop things in that box from all over, Hattie. Could be from last month's guests, for all I know.\\\"\\n\\nHattie copied the date carefully into her notebook. Somebody, it seemed, had been in Pennywhistle Harbor a day sooner than anyone had said.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Dottie\",\n        \"cupboard\",\n        \"glove\",\n        \"sunglasses\",\n        \"shovel\",\n        \"umbrellas\",\n        \"paperback\",\n        \"hairbrush\",\n        \"ticket\",\n        \"Boston\",\n        \"Portland\",\n        \"scones\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 17,\n      \"title\": \"Twelve Years of Tea\",\n      \"text\": \"Hattie found Colette Marsh on the inn's back porch late Sunday afternoon, wrapped in a gray cardigan and staring out at the garden. A cup of tea sat untouched on the rail beside her. She looked smaller than ever, Hattie thought, like a sparrow that had lost its flock.\\n\\n\\\"May I sit?\\\" Hattie asked, and Colette nodded. Then Colette said softly, \\\"Twelve years. I worked for him for twelve years. I knew exactly how he liked everything. Darjeeling, steeped four minutes, the cup warmed first. Never milk, never sugar. I typed his columns, answered his letters, and tested his recipes until midnight.\\\" She smiled faintly. \\\"People thought he was cruel. He could be. But he was my whole job, my whole life.\\\"\\n\\nHattie gently asked where she had been on the morning of the bake-off. Colette did not seem to mind the question. \\\"At the library,\\\" she said. \\\"Lionel wanted a stack of old regional cookbooks for his research, so I went to fetch them first thing. By the time I reached the green, it was all over.\\\" Her voice wobbled, and she dabbed her eyes with a folded handkerchief.\\n\\nHattie squeezed her hand. Biscuit, who had followed her up the steps, settled against Colette's ankle, and she stroked him absently with neat, careful fingers. The garden rustled in the cool breeze below them.\\n\\nThat evening Hattie wrote down every word, the way she wrote down everything, and wondered what twelve years of loyalty might feel like.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Colette\",\n        \"porch\",\n        \"cardigan\",\n        \"sparrow\",\n        \"Darjeeling\",\n        \"columns\",\n        \"midnight\",\n        \"library\",\n        \"cookbooks\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"ankle\",\n        \"breeze\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 18,\n      \"title\": \"Recipes in the Margins\",\n      \"text\": \"Monday morning was the busiest the Rolling Pin had seen all week. Half the village seemed to want pumpkin bread, and a cluster of guests from the Gull's Rest Inn crowded in for coffee before their trains home. Hattie rang up orders until her feet ached, and Biscuit wisely hid under the counter.\\n\\nWhen the rush finally ended, she found a worn leather notebook left behind on the table by the window. It had no name inside the cover, only page after page of handwritten recipes in faded blue ink. Maple walnut tart. Cranberry orange scones. A brown butter pound cake with a pinch of cardamom. The margins were crowded with tiny notes like \\\"less salt\\\" and \\\"bake five minutes longer\\\" and \\\"try with lemon zest.\\\"\\n\\nSomething tugged at Hattie's memory. She fetched her own copy of Lionel Crane's famous cookbook from the shelf behind the register, the one with his smug photograph on the cover. She turned the pages slowly, then turned them again. The maple walnut tart was there, word for word. So were the scones and the pound cake, right down to the cardamom.\\n\\nHattie sat very still. Either someone had copied Lionel's recipes into a notebook by hand, with years of careful notes in the margins, or the recipes had been written here first.\\n\\nShe wrapped the notebook in a clean tea towel and tucked it safely behind the counter. Whoever had left it would surely come back for it, and she meant to return it herself.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"pumpkin\",\n        \"notebook\",\n        \"leather\",\n        \"margins\",\n        \"maple\",\n        \"walnut\",\n        \"cranberry\",\n        \"cardamom\",\n        \"register\",\n        \"photograph\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"counter\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 19,\n      \"title\": \"A Nasty Note\",\n      \"text\": \"Tuesday dawned cold and bright, and Hattie was halfway through her first batch of croissants when Winnie Calloway rapped on the bakery's back door. \\\"You had better come,\\\" Winnie said, pulling her shawl tight. \\\"It's Delia.\\\"\\n\\nAcross the square, a small crowd had gathered outside the Sugar and Spoon. Taped to the front window was a sheet of paper with a single cruel word printed in thick black marker, followed by a line telling Delia to pack up and leave town. Delia Fairweather stood on the sidewalk in her flour-dusted smock, arms folded tight, chin trembling. \\\"I have baked for this village for six years,\\\" she said quietly. \\\"Six years, and now this.\\\"\\n\\nHattie did not waste a moment. She marched back for a bucket of warm soapy water, two sponges, and a bottle of vinegar for the sticky tape. Winnie brought three mugs of cocoa from her bookshop and handed them round. The crowd, a little ashamed, began to drift away. Together the three women peeled and scrubbed until the glass sparkled and not a scrap remained.\\n\\nBefore she threw the paper away, Hattie folded it neatly and slipped it into her coat pocket. The printing was careful and blocky, the sort of hand someone uses when they do not want their writing known. \\\"Whoever did this,\\\" she told Delia firmly, \\\"is a coward. And cowards make mistakes.\\\"\\n\\nDelia managed a watery smile and wrapped both hands around her cocoa. \\\"Then I hope you catch one, Hattie.\\\"\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"croissants\",\n        \"Winnie\",\n        \"Calloway\",\n        \"shawl\",\n        \"Delia\",\n        \"marker\",\n        \"sidewalk\",\n        \"bucket\",\n        \"sponges\",\n        \"vinegar\",\n        \"cocoa\",\n        \"coward\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 20,\n      \"title\": \"Biscuit Knocks a Jar\",\n      \"text\": \"Wednesday night the bakery was warm and quiet, and Hattie was mixing a double batch of spice cake for the church supper. The radio hummed softly, the oven ticked as it heated, and Biscuit prowled along the top of the shelves where he was not allowed to be.\\n\\nShe had just set the kitchen timer when she heard a clink, a wobble, and a crash. A glass jar of nutmeg had rolled off the shelf and burst on the floor, scattering brown powder from the sink to the pantry. Biscuit peered down at the mess innocently. \\\"You rascal,\\\" Hattie scolded, reaching for the broom. The kitchen filled with a warm, sharp fragrance.\\n\\nAs she swept, Hattie thought about spice cake. Nobody tasted the nutmeg the moment it went into the bowl. The flavor bloomed slowly, in the oven, long after the mixing was done. By the time the timer rang, the real work had happened an hour before. The timer only told you when to look.\\n\\nShe stopped sweeping. Everyone in the village had watched Lionel bite into Delia's plum cake and collapse soon after, so naturally they all blamed the cake. But what if the cake was only the timer ringing? What if the trouble had begun much earlier that morning, in a place where nobody was watching?\\n\\nHattie leaned the broom against the counter and reached for her green notebook. Biscuit hopped down, rubbed against her ankles, and purred as if he had planned it all along.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"shelves\",\n        \"timer\",\n        \"nutmeg\",\n        \"powder\",\n        \"pantry\",\n        \"rascal\",\n        \"broom\",\n        \"fragrance\",\n        \"flavor\",\n        \"supper\",\n        \"Lionel\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ]\n}","{\r\n  \"chapters\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 21,\r\n      \"title\": \"A Package for a Dead Man\",\r\n      \"text\": \"The bell over the bookshop door jingled just after ten, and a courier in a damp brown jacket shuffled in with a parcel tucked under his arm. Winnie Calloway looked up from her cocoa and her crossword. Hattie Bramble, who had popped next door with a plate of cinnamon twists, set the plate down at once.\\n\\n\\\"Delivery for Mr. Lionel Crane,\\\" the courier said, reading from his clipboard. \\\"The inn sent me here. They said the bookshop takes in packages for guests.\\\" Winnie and Hattie exchanged a look. Nobody had told the poor young man. Winnie signed for it with a sigh, and the courier hurried back out into the drizzle, none the wiser.\\n\\nThe parcel came from a publisher in New York. Hattie knew perfectly well that it ought to go straight to Sam. Still, the flap had come unstuck in the rain, and a thick sheaf of papers had slid halfway out on its own. It seemed only polite to tuck them back in, and impossible not to read the first page while doing so.\\n\\nIt was a contract for a brand new cookbook, to be written by Lionel Crane and Lionel Crane alone. There was no mention of an assistant, a helper, or anyone else at all. Biscuit, who had followed Hattie over, sat on the counter and sniffed the corner of the package.\\n\\n\\\"Whose recipes, I wonder,\\\" Hattie murmured, and she copied the title into her little green notebook.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Winnie\",\r\n        \"contract\",\r\n        \"courier\",\r\n        \"parcel\",\r\n        \"bookshop\",\r\n        \"cookbook\",\r\n        \"cocoa\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"publisher\",\r\n        \"drizzle\",\r\n        \"clipboard\",\r\n        \"cinnamon\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 22,\r\n      \"title\": \"The Harbor Master Confesses\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Hattie found Gordon Pike in the harbor office, staring at a mug of coffee that had long since gone cold. Outside, the gulls wheeled over the fishing boats, and a foghorn moaned from somewhere past the lighthouse. She set a warm blueberry muffin on his desk and waited. Thirty years of teaching had taught her that silence was often the best question.\\n\\nGordon lasted nearly a full minute. \\\"It was the year my sister Margaret was so ill,\\\" he said at last, rubbing his weathered face. \\\"She had entered her apple pie in the bake-off every autumn for twenty years and never won so much as a ribbon. I was on the committee. I moved a few numbers on the scorecards. She went home with the blue ribbon, and she was so happy, Hattie. She passed that winter.\\\" Hattie's heart softened, though her pencil kept moving in her green notebook.\\n\\n\\\"Lionel found out,\\\" Gordon went on. \\\"Heaven knows how. He had a nose for scandal. He kept hinting he would mention it on his television show unless I made certain the committee did whatever he wanted. Better rooms, better seats, and his own way in everything.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"And now he cannot mention anything at all,\\\" Hattie said gently.\\n\\nGordon flushed red to his ears. \\\"I know how that sounds. I did not hurt the man. But I will not pretend I am sorry he is quiet.\\\" He looked away, toward the rowboats bobbing by the pier.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Gordon\",\r\n        \"harbor\",\r\n        \"coffee\",\r\n        \"gulls\",\r\n        \"foghorn\",\r\n        \"lighthouse\",\r\n        \"blueberry\",\r\n        \"sister\",\r\n        \"ribbon\",\r\n        \"scorecards\",\r\n        \"television\",\r\n        \"rowboats\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 23,\r\n      \"title\": \"Words Never Sent\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Dottie Quill had been asked to air out the Blue Suite, and she did not care to do it alone. \\\"It gives me the shivers, Hattie,\\\" she confessed, handing over a feather duster. \\\"All his things are still here, just as he left them.\\\"\\n\\nDetective Hask had already carried off his suitcase and his travel kettle. What remained was a stack of glossy magazines, a silk dressing gown, and the old walnut writing desk by the window. Hattie dusted it slowly. One of the small drawers stuck, and when she gave it a patient wiggle, it slid open to reveal a single sheet of cream stationery.\\n\\nIt was a letter in Lionel's sharp, slanting hand, crossed out in places. It was addressed to Colette. \\\"After twelve years,\\\" it read, \\\"I have decided your services are no longer required. You will vacate your position at the end of the month. I trust you will not make a fuss.\\\" It was unsigned and undated, and it had plainly never been sent.\\n\\nHattie read it twice, and her throat tightened. What a cold way to end twelve years of anyone's life. Still, she reminded herself, it was one motive among several. Rupert had his debts, Gordon had his secret, Imogen had her inheritance, and poor Delia had her old grudge. She copied the letter word for word into her green notebook and slid the sheet back into its drawer.\\n\\n\\\"Find anything?\\\" Dottie called from the hallway. \\\"Something Sam ought to see,\\\" Hattie answered quietly.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"letter\",\r\n        \"Dottie\",\r\n        \"duster\",\r\n        \"walnut\",\r\n        \"magazines\",\r\n        \"stationery\",\r\n        \"drawer\",\r\n        \"Colette\",\r\n        \"Rupert\",\r\n        \"Imogen\",\r\n        \"Gordon\",\r\n        \"hallway\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 24,\r\n      \"title\": \"Stay Out of It, Aunt Hattie\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Deputy Sam Bramble came through the back door of the Rolling Pin just as Hattie pulled a tray of apple turnovers from the oven. He took off his hat, which was never a good sign. Sam only took off his hat when he had something serious to say. \\\"Aunt Hattie,\\\" he began, \\\"I need you to stop poking around.\\\"\\n\\nHattie slid a turnover onto a plate and set it in front of him. \\\"Poking? I deliver pastries, dear. I chat. It is called being neighborly.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Detective Hask doesn't call it that.\\\" Sam sighed and broke the turnover in half, letting the steam curl up. \\\"She's heard you've been in the potting shed, the laundry room, and now the Blue Suite. She asked me whether my aunt was running a private detective agency out of a bakery kitchen.\\\" Biscuit hopped onto the stool beside him and eyed the turnover hopefully.\\n\\n\\\"The county detective has made up her mind about Delia,\\\" Hattie said quietly. \\\"That is exactly why someone needs to keep an open one.\\\"\\n\\nSam rubbed the back of his neck. \\\"I know you mean well. But if you muddy things up, it could hurt Delia more than help her. Lorna Hask is sharp, and she plays by the book. Please, just bake your pies for a few days.\\\" Hattie patted his hand and refilled his coffee. She did not promise anything, and Sam, who had known her his whole life, noticed that she did not.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Deputy\",\r\n        \"turnovers\",\r\n        \"Hask\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"county\",\r\n        \"detective\",\r\n        \"Delia\",\r\n        \"coffee\",\r\n        \"kitchen\",\r\n        \"neighborly\",\r\n        \"stool\",\r\n        \"steam\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 25,\r\n      \"title\": \"The Innkeeper's Fib\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Rupert Ashby was polishing brass in the front hall of the Gull's Rest Inn, rubbing the same doorknob over and over as if it had personally offended him. He gave Hattie a tired smile when she came in with the morning bread. \\\"Terrible week,\\\" he said. \\\"Guests are canceling left and right. Nobody wants to sleep where a famous man was poisoned.\\\" When Hattie gently pointed out that Lionel had fallen ill at the tea tent, not here, he only shook his head. \\\"Try telling that to the newspapers.\\\" He set down his rag. \\\"I've told the detective a dozen times. I was right here all morning, in my office, doing the accounts. I never left the building until the judging started.\\\"\\n\\nHattie tilted her head. She remembered that morning perfectly. She had arrived at dawn with a basket of croissants and rung the little silver bell on the front desk three times. Nobody had answered. And when she had walked back down the gravel drive, Rupert's green station wagon had been missing from its usual spot beside the hedge. \\\"How odd,\\\" she said mildly. \\\"I must have just missed you, then.\\\"\\n\\nA flush crept up Rupert's neck. He began to scrub at the doorknob with new energy. \\\"I might have stepped out for a moment. Errands. I don't recall exactly.\\\"\\n\\nHattie nodded as though that were the most natural thing in the world. Back on the sidewalk, she opened her green notebook and underlined his name twice.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Rupert\",\r\n        \"brass\",\r\n        \"doorknob\",\r\n        \"newspapers\",\r\n        \"accounts\",\r\n        \"croissants\",\r\n        \"gravel\",\r\n        \"wagon\",\r\n        \"hedge\",\r\n        \"errands\",\r\n        \"detective\",\r\n        \"basket\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 26,\r\n      \"title\": \"A Scent in the Shed\",\r\n      \"text\": \"The potting shed smelled of damp earth and old clay pots, just as it had the first time. Hattie stepped inside with Biscuit at her heels and pulled the door nearly closed behind her. Rain drummed softly on the tin roof.\\n\\nShe was not entirely sure what she was looking for. Something about the empty hook had been nagging at her for days, the way a missing ingredient nags at a recipe. She stood very still beside the workbench and let her eyes wander over the coiled hoses, the seed trays, the cracked watering can, and the hook itself, still bare.\\n\\nThen she noticed it. Beneath the earthy smell was something softer and sweeter, the faint perfume of lavender. It was not the garden kind, which grew by the stone wall outside and had long since gone to seed. This was the creamy, powdery scent of a good hand cream, the sort that came in a little tin and cost more than it ought to.\\n\\nHattie bent close to the workbench. The smell was strongest near a shelf of garden twine and on the doorframe, where someone might have steadied themselves. Someone with soft hands, she thought, who had cared enough to protect them before coming out to work among the thorns.\\n\\nBiscuit sneezed twice, offended. \\\"I agree,\\\" Hattie whispered. \\\"Gardeners don't usually smell like a fancy shop.\\\" She wrote the word lavender in her notebook and drew a small circle around it.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"lavender\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"workbench\",\r\n        \"hoses\",\r\n        \"watering\",\r\n        \"perfume\",\r\n        \"cream\",\r\n        \"thorns\",\r\n        \"twine\",\r\n        \"doorframe\",\r\n        \"roof\",\r\n        \"clay\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 27,\r\n      \"title\": \"A Fisherman's Word\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Abe Tolliver had been mending nets on the same weathered bench by the harbor for as long as Hattie could remember. He was eighty-one, deaf in one ear, and sharp as a fishhook in every other way. Hattie sat down beside him with a thermos of hot cider and two molasses cookies wrapped in wax paper. \\\"You want to know about the painter girl,\\\" Abe said before she could even ask. \\\"Whole village does.\\\"\\n\\nHattie admitted that she did. Imogen claimed she had been painting at the lighthouse that morning, and Abe always went out early. Abe chewed a cookie slowly, thinking. \\\"Saw her, all right. Quarter past five, I'd say. I was rowing out to check my lobster traps, and there she was on the rocks with her easel, bundled up in that yellow slicker of hers. Hard to miss.\\\"\\n\\nHattie's heart lifted, but Abe wagged a crooked finger before she could say a word. \\\"Don't go thinking she was there the whole morning. I was out past the point an hour, maybe more. When I came back in, the easel was still standing there, but I didn't see hide nor hair of her. Could have been inside the lighthouse. Could have been anywhere.\\\"\\n\\nHattie wrote it down carefully: seen at five, then not seen for at least an hour. An easel, after all, could stand by itself.\\n\\n\\\"Thank you, Abe,\\\" she said, pouring him more cider. He grunted and went back to his nets, humming an old sea shanty.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Tolliver\",\r\n        \"thermos\",\r\n        \"cider\",\r\n        \"molasses\",\r\n        \"cookies\",\r\n        \"Imogen\",\r\n        \"lighthouse\",\r\n        \"lobster\",\r\n        \"traps\",\r\n        \"easel\",\r\n        \"slicker\",\r\n        \"shanty\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 28,\r\n      \"title\": \"Biscuit Digs In\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Biscuit had never cared for leashes, but he tolerated his red harness on the walk past the Gull's Rest Inn, mostly because the walled garden was full of interesting smells. This morning he was more interested than usual. The moment Hattie let him through the gate, he tugged her straight toward a large blue hydrangea by the far wall.\\n\\n\\\"What is it, sweetheart?\\\" Hattie asked. \\\"A mouse?\\\" Biscuit did not answer, being a cat. Instead he began to dig with great determination, flinging damp soil and brown petals onto Hattie's good walking shoes. She tried to tug him away, then stopped. The earth beneath the bush was loose and dark, as if someone had turned it over very recently.\\n\\nSomething metal glinted among the roots. Hattie knelt, wincing as her knees complained, and drew it out with two fingers wrapped in her handkerchief. It was a small garden trowel with a wooden handle, caked in mud. Someone had pushed it deep under the hydrangea, where nobody would ever think to look.\\n\\nHattie turned it over slowly. Bits of green leaf clung to the blade. She could not be sure, but they looked a great deal like the foxglove she had seen cut down by the wall.\\n\\nShe wrapped the trowel carefully and tucked it into her basket. Sam would scold her, but he would scold her far more if she left it there for the rain. \\\"Good boy,\\\" she told Biscuit, who accepted the praise and a sardine treat as his due.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"harness\",\r\n        \"garden\",\r\n        \"hydrangea\",\r\n        \"petals\",\r\n        \"shoes\",\r\n        \"roots\",\r\n        \"trowel\",\r\n        \"handle\",\r\n        \"foxglove\",\r\n        \"basket\",\r\n        \"sardine\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 29,\r\n      \"title\": \"The Village Rallies\",\r\n      \"text\": \"The news spread across Pennywhistle Harbor before the church bell finished striking noon. Detective Lorna Hask had named Delia Fairweather a person of interest. By one o'clock, the Sugar and Spoon was as empty as a cookie jar after a church picnic.\\n\\nHattie watched from the window of the Rolling Pin, her hands on her hips. Across the square, Delia stood alone behind her glass counter, arranging and rearranging a tray of maple scones that nobody had come to buy. \\\"That will not do,\\\" Hattie announced to no one in particular. Biscuit flicked an ear.\\n\\nShe marched next door to the bookshop, where Winnie Calloway was already reaching for her coat. \\\"I know that look,\\\" Winnie said. \\\"Who are we calling first?\\\"\\n\\nThey called everyone. Winnie rang the knitting circle, the garden club, and her cousin who ran the post office. Hattie rang Oswald Finch at the library, who promised to bring the entire Thursday reading group. She even put a hand-lettered sign in her own window: Rolling Pin closed for one hour. Go buy a scone from Delia.\\n\\nBy three o'clock there was a line out the door of the Sugar and Spoon. Abe Tolliver bought six cinnamon buns. Dottie Quill bought a whole pie. Oswald Finch bought a lemon tart. Delia caught Hattie's eye through the crowd, her own eyes shining. Hattie simply winked. Some things a village could fix with a pie or two, even when it could not fix everything.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Delia\",\r\n        \"Hask\",\r\n        \"picnic\",\r\n        \"scones\",\r\n        \"counter\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"Winnie\",\r\n        \"knitting\",\r\n        \"Oswald\",\r\n        \"cinnamon\",\r\n        \"Dottie\",\r\n        \"lemon\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 30,\r\n      \"title\": \"Small Muddy Boots\",\r\n      \"text\": \"It was nearly dusk when Hattie carried a box of day-old bread around to the back of the Gull's Rest Inn. Dottie liked to feed the crumbs to the sparrows, and Hattie hated waste. The back porch was a cluttered, friendly place, with a rocking chair, a stack of firewood, and a row of pegs for coats and umbrellas. Hattie set the box down by the screen door and was about to knock when something made her pause.\\n\\nOn a folded newspaper beneath the bench sat a pair of rubber garden boots, set out to dry. They were caked to the ankle in thick dark mud, the very same reddish mud that clung to the beds of the walled garden. What struck Hattie most was their size. They were small, much smaller than her own, the sort of boots that might fit a slender woman or a girl of twelve.\\n\\nRupert's feet were enormous. Gordon Pike wore size thirteen and complained about it constantly. Lionel, she recalled, had worn polished loafers and never set foot in soil if he could help it. Hattie leaned closer without touching them. A crushed purple petal was stuck to one heel.\\n\\n\\\"Those aren't mine,\\\" Dottie said, pushing open the screen door with a tea towel over her shoulder. \\\"Been sitting there since the bake-off. I assumed one of the guests left them.\\\" Hattie smiled and handed her the bread. Then she wrote two words, small boots, in her notebook and underlined them.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"boots\",\r\n        \"Dottie\",\r\n        \"sparrows\",\r\n        \"porch\",\r\n        \"firewood\",\r\n        \"umbrellas\",\r\n        \"newspaper\",\r\n        \"ankle\",\r\n        \"Rupert\",\r\n        \"loafers\",\r\n        \"petal\",\r\n        \"towel\"\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}","{\r\n  \"chapters\": [\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 31,\r\n      \"title\": \"Rupert Comes Clean\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Rupert Ashby found Hattie on Wednesday morning at the bakery counter, his cap twisted in both hands like a wet dishrag. Biscuit took one look at him from the windowsill and turned his orange back. \\\"I lied to you,\\\" Rupert said. \\\"About the morning of the bake-off. I wasn't fixing the furnace at the inn. I was in Bangor, sitting across a desk from a bank officer.\\\"\\n\\nHattie set down a tray of cinnamon buns and reached for her green notebook. \\\"Why on earth keep that a secret?\\\" Rupert's ears went pink. \\\"Because the whole village would know by lunchtime that the Gull's Rest is drowning in debt. I was begging for a loan so I would never have to sell to Lionel Crane. I left before sunrise and didn't get back until the tea tent was already in an uproar.\\\"\\n\\nHe slid a folded card across the counter. It was an appointment card from the Coastal Savings Bank, with the date and the hour stamped in purple ink. \\\"Mr. Pruitt will vouch for me,\\\" he said.\\n\\nHattie studied the card, then the man. He looked tired, but he did not look like someone hiding a darker secret. Still, she copied the bank's telephone number into her notebook. \\\"Did you get the loan?\\\" she asked gently. \\\"Not yet,\\\" Rupert admitted. \\\"But I would rather lose the inn honestly than keep it with a lie.\\\" Hattie wrapped him a warm cinnamon bun for the road.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Rupert\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"Hattie\",\r\n        \"dishrag\",\r\n        \"windowsill\",\r\n        \"Bangor\",\r\n        \"cinnamon\",\r\n        \"furnace\",\r\n        \"village\",\r\n        \"sunrise\",\r\n        \"purple\",\r\n        \"Pruitt\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 32,\r\n      \"title\": \"Candlelight and Clues\",\r\n      \"text\": \"By Thursday evening a nor'easter came howling up the coast, rattling the bakery shutters and flinging rain at the glass. At half past seven the lights flickered twice and went out, and the whole village square sank into darkness. Biscuit gave an offended yowl from under the table.\\n\\nWinnie Calloway arrived ten minutes later, dripping from her yellow slicker and carrying two thermoses of cocoa. \\\"The bookshop is black as a coal bin,\\\" she announced. \\\"I thought we might as well be dark together.\\\" Hattie lit every candle she owned, set them along the counter, and opened her green notebook to a fresh page.\\n\\nOne by one they went through the clues. The cut foxglove in the walled garden. The empty hook in the potting shed. The apron with green stains, the honey in a teacup belonging to a man who hated honey, the early train ticket, and the recipe notebook that matched Lionel's cookbook word for word. Then the contract, the unsent letter, the whiff of lavender, the muddy trowel and the small boots on the back porch.\\n\\n\\\"It's like a quilt with half the squares missing,\\\" Winnie said, licking cocoa from her lip. Winnie loved a good list almost as much as a good rumor,. \\\"Meanwhile poor Delia gets all the blame.\\\" Hattie nodded slowly. \\\"Every piece belongs somewhere. We just have to see the pattern.\\\" Outside, the wind moaned around the chimney, and the candle flames bent and straightened as if they were listening too.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Winnie\",\r\n        \"Calloway\",\r\n        \"thermoses\",\r\n        \"cocoa\",\r\n        \"slicker\",\r\n        \"candle\",\r\n        \"foxglove\",\r\n        \"quilt\",\r\n        \"chimney\",\r\n        \"shutters\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"pattern\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 33,\r\n      \"title\": \"Leaves in a Guidebook\",\r\n      \"text\": \"The morning after the storm, the Gull's Rest Inn smelled of wet wool and woodsmoke. Dottie Quill had asked Hattie to help tidy the reading room, where a leak had dripped onto the window seat and soaked a row of paperbacks. Hattie spread the damp books across the radiator.\\n\\nNear the end of the shelf sat a fat guidebook called Coastal Walks of Maine, its spine cracked from years of use. When Hattie lifted it, something slid out from between the pages and landed on the rug with a soft rustle. It was a small brown envelope, the kind seed packets come in, folded over twice and held shut with a paper clip.\\n\\nHattie opened it carefully over her handkerchief. Inside were crushed dried leaves, gray-green and brittle, with a faint bitter smell that made her nose wrinkle. \\\"Dottie, do you know who's been reading this book?\\\" Dottie squinted at it. \\\"Anybody. Guests borrow from these shelves all week and never sign anything out. Mr. Crane's people were in here, and Mr. Ashby, and half the committee came through for sherry on Sunday.\\\"\\n\\nHattie folded the handkerchief around the envelope and tucked it into her handbag. She wrote the title of the guidebook in her notebook, along with the page where it had been hidden, a map of the cliff path above the harbor. Something about those leaves made her uneasy, and she meant to show them to Sam before the day was out.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"envelope\",\r\n        \"Dottie\",\r\n        \"woodsmoke\",\r\n        \"radiator\",\r\n        \"guidebook\",\r\n        \"rustle\",\r\n        \"handbag\",\r\n        \"sherry\",\r\n        \"brittle\",\r\n        \"cliff\",\r\n        \"paperbacks\",\r\n        \"harbor\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 34,\r\n      \"title\": \"Clear Skies for Gordon\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Hattie walked down to the harbor office on Friday with a bag of molasses cookies. Gordon Pike was at his desk beneath a wall of tide charts, a mug of black coffee cooling by his elbow.\\n\\n\\\"I know what folks are saying,\\\" he grumbled. \\\"That I had a reason to want Lionel quiet. Well, I did. But I never left this office the whole morning of the bake-off.\\\" He pulled a battered ledger from a drawer and flipped it open. \\\"The radio log. Every call in and out of this harbor gets written down with the time, and I signed every single entry.\\\"\\n\\nHattie ran her finger down the column. There was Gordon's blocky handwriting at six fifteen, talking a lobster boat in through the fog. Again at seven, and seven thirty, and every quarter hour after that, right up until the siren wailed from the green. Abe Tolliver's name appeared twice, calling in from the Mary Ellen to complain about a tangled buoy line.\\n\\n\\\"Abe will tell you I answered him myself,\\\" Gordon said. Hattie smiled despite herself. Abe was the most honest grouch in Pennywhistle Harbor. She thanked Gordon, left him the rest of the cookies, and walked back up the hill with the sea wind tugging at her scarf. In her green notebook she drew a neat line through his name. It felt good to cross someone off, even if the list was still far too long.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Gordon\",\r\n        \"molasses\",\r\n        \"cookies\",\r\n        \"coffee\",\r\n        \"ledger\",\r\n        \"lobster\",\r\n        \"buoy\",\r\n        \"Tolliver\",\r\n        \"grouch\",\r\n        \"scarf\",\r\n        \"siren\",\r\n        \"charts\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 35,\r\n      \"title\": \"Left Behind in the Tent\",\r\n      \"text\": \"The striped tea tent still stood on the village green, sagging a little after the storm. The committee had been too rattled to take it down, and a long trestle table near the back held crates of borrowed crockery waiting to be returned to their owners. Hattie had promised to sort it all, since half the cups came from her own cupboards.\\n\\nShe worked down the row with Biscuit supervising from a folding chair. But tucked in the bottom of the last crate, wrapped in a crumpled napkin, was a single white saucer with a gold rim. It had never been washed at all. A thin brown ring of dried tea clung to the center.\\n\\nHattie knew that pattern. It matched the monogrammed cup Lionel carried everywhere, the one Colette had unpacked so carefully at the inn. Somebody had cleared the judges' table in a rush and missed it. She did not touch it with her fingers, but lifted it with the napkin and set it gently into an empty cake tin.\\n\\nDeputy Sam Bramble arrived within the quarter hour. He peered into the tin and let out a low whistle. \\\"Aunt Hattie, the county crew went over this tent twice.\\\" \\\"Then they looked at the cups and forgot the saucers,\\\" Hattie said. \\\"Nobody ever looks at the saucer.\\\" Sam sealed it in an evidence bag, and promised it would go to the state lab in Augusta that very afternoon.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"saucer\",\r\n        \"crockery\",\r\n        \"trestle\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"napkin\",\r\n        \"crumpled\",\r\n        \"Colette\",\r\n        \"Deputy\",\r\n        \"whistle\",\r\n        \"Augusta\",\r\n        \"cupboards\",\r\n        \"evidence\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 36,\r\n      \"title\": \"Stick to Pies\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Hattie unlocked the Rolling Pin at five, switched on the ovens, and tied on her flour-dusted smock. Then she saw it: a folded square of paper lying just inside the front door, as if someone had slid it underneath.\\n\\nBiscuit reached it first and batted it across the tiles. The message was printed in stiff capital letters, the kind a person uses when they do not want their handwriting known. It said: STICK TO PIES, MRS. BRAMBLE. LEAVE THE REST ALONE. There was no name at the bottom, only a smudge of something that might have been ink or might have been soot.\\n\\nHattie sat down hard on the stool behind the counter. Her hands were steady, but her heart was thumping like a mixer on high. In thirty years of teaching she had received plenty of anonymous notes, mostly about homework, and not one of them had frightened her. This one came close.\\n\\nWhen Winnie came over with the morning cocoa, she read it twice and turned pale. \\\"Hattie, you have to show Sam. Somebody is watching you.\\\" \\\"Somebody is worried,\\\" Hattie corrected, though she bolted the back door all the same. She slipped the note into a clean bread bag for Sam and copied every word into her green notebook. Then she rolled out the pastry for the apple pies. If someone wanted her to stick to pies, she would make the best pies they had ever tasted, and keep asking questions while they cooled.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"ovens\",\r\n        \"smock\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"tiles\",\r\n        \"capital\",\r\n        \"Bramble\",\r\n        \"smudge\",\r\n        \"stool\",\r\n        \"mixer\",\r\n        \"homework\",\r\n        \"Winnie\",\r\n        \"pastry\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 37,\r\n      \"title\": \"Who Knew the Schedule\",\r\n      \"text\": \"On Monday Gordon Pike sent up a cardboard folder from the harbor office with a note that read, For whatever good it does. Inside were the committee papers from the bake-off: entry forms, ribbon orders, and the seating chart for the judges' table. Hattie spread them across her kitchen table while Biscuit sat on the parts she most needed to read.\\n\\nThe public program, the one pinned up all over the village, listed only the categories: breads, pies, preserves, cakes. But at the very back of the folder was a single typed sheet marked PRIVATE, the official judging timetable. It gave every entry a number and an exact time. Entry fourteen, plum cake, Sugar and Spoon, ten forty.\\n\\nHattie sat back and frowned. Gordon had penciled a line along the margin: Only three copies printed, one for each judge and their people. Nobody else saw it. That meant the baker herself had not known when her cake would be tasted. The crowd had not known, nor the reporters. Only the judges and the folks who fetched their tea and kept their papers in order.\\n\\nShe thought of Lionel's travel kettle, the honey in his cup, and the long hour before the tasting began. Whoever wanted the blame to fall on Delia would have needed to know precisely when that plum cake reached the table. Hattie underlined the word timetable twice in her green notebook. Biscuit yawned, unimpressed, and shifted onto the seating chart.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"timetable\",\r\n        \"cardboard\",\r\n        \"ribbon\",\r\n        \"seating\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"program\",\r\n        \"preserves\",\r\n        \"plum\",\r\n        \"margin\",\r\n        \"reporters\",\r\n        \"kettle\",\r\n        \"Delia\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 38,\r\n      \"title\": \"The Will That Wasn't\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Imogen Vale came into the Rolling Pin on Tuesday with blue paint on her cuffs and a lawyer's folder under her arm. She ordered a pumpkin muffin, did not eat it, and finally sat down across from Hattie at the corner table by the window.\\n\\n\\\"I owe you the truth,\\\" Imogen said. \\\"When I told you I would inherit Uncle Lionel's house, I was showing off. The truth is that he never changed his will. He wrote it thirty years ago, and it leaves every penny and every brick to a cooking school scholarship in Portland.\\\" She slid the papers across. \\\"His lawyer sent me a copy last spring, because I asked. I already knew I would get nothing but his old easel.\\\"\\n\\nHattie read the lawyer's letterhead, then looked up. Imogen's eyes were bright, but she held her chin steady. \\\"He used to promise me the house when I was little,\\\" she said. \\\"I stopped believing him a long time ago.\\\" Hattie reached across and patted her paint-speckled hand.\\n\\nIf Imogen had known since spring that she would inherit nothing, then she had no reason to hurry her uncle along. Hattie wrote it carefully in her green notebook and drew a small box around it. The list of suspects was getting shorter, and the shadows around the ones who remained seemed to grow longer every day. She pushed the pumpkin muffin back toward Imogen and told her to eat something.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"Imogen\",\r\n        \"cuffs\",\r\n        \"pumpkin\",\r\n        \"muffin\",\r\n        \"Lionel\",\r\n        \"penny\",\r\n        \"brick\",\r\n        \"Portland\",\r\n        \"lawyer\",\r\n        \"easel\",\r\n        \"chin\",\r\n        \"shadows\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 39,\r\n      \"title\": \"Shelves of Seed Catalogs\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Rain drummed on the roof all Wednesday afternoon, so Hattie took a thermos of tea and her notebook to the shared reading room at the Gull's Rest Inn. Dottie Quill had lit a small fire in the grate. The room smelled of old paper, and the lemon oil Dottie rubbed into the shelves every Friday.\\n\\nThe gardening section ran along the whole back wall. There were seed catalogs from the 1950s with hand-tinted pictures of giant tomatoes, a field guide to Maine wildflowers, and a crumbling almanac. Hattie pulled them down one at a time and turned the pages slowly, looking for anything that did not belong.\\n\\nShe learned a good deal more than she expected. Foxglove, one book said, was a handsome biennial that loved cool shade and should never be planted where children or pets might nibble it. Another book had a whole chapter on cottage gardens with a careful drawing of the inn's own walled garden, labeled in faded ink. Biscuit, who had followed her over, curled up on a velvet cushion and snored.\\n\\nDottie came by with a plate of shortbread and peered at the stack. \\\"Nobody's touched those in years,\\\" she said. Then she paused. \\\"Well, almost nobody. A few of the guests were in here last week, browsing the plant books. I didn't pay much mind to who.\\\" Hattie nodded and kept reading, the fire popping softly beside her, until the windows went blue with evening.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"thermos\",\r\n        \"Dottie\",\r\n        \"grate\",\r\n        \"lemon\",\r\n        \"catalogs\",\r\n        \"tomatoes\",\r\n        \"almanac\",\r\n        \"biennial\",\r\n        \"velvet\",\r\n        \"cushion\",\r\n        \"shortbread\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\"\r\n      ]\r\n    },\r\n    {\r\n      \"n\": 40,\r\n      \"title\": \"A Pressed Leaf\",\r\n      \"text\": \"Hattie came back to the reading room on Thursday morning, determined to finish the gardening shelf. Near the bottom, wedged behind the almanac, she found a slim green volume she had missed the day before: A Practical Guide to Medicinal Plants, printed in Boston in 1931. Its cloth cover was worn soft at the corners, but the spine was not dusty at all. Someone had handled it recently.\\n\\nShe opened it on her lap, and the book fell open by itself. Lying across the page was a single pressed leaf, flat and papery, with a fuzzy underside and toothed edges. Hattie knew it at once. It was a foxglove leaf, carefully dried and slipped between the pages as a bookmark.\\n\\nThe chapter it marked was titled Digitalis and the Heart. Hattie felt a chill. The words described how a few leaves, steeped like tea, could slow a heart until it stopped. There was a small pencil tick in the margin.\\n\\nShe did not pull the leaf free. Instead she slid her own handkerchief beneath the book, closed it gently, and wrapped it in a clean dish towel from her bag. Biscuit watched her from the hearth with round, solemn eyes. Anyone at the inn could have walked into this room and opened this book, and Hattie reminded herself of that firmly. Still, as she walked down the hill toward Sam's office, she could not stop wondering whose patient hands had pressed that leaf, and why.\",\r\n      \"words\": [\r\n        \"bookmark\",\r\n        \"almanac\",\r\n        \"Boston\",\r\n        \"cloth\",\r\n        \"spine\",\r\n        \"foxglove\",\r\n        \"papery\",\r\n        \"Digitalis\",\r\n        \"pencil\",\r\n        \"margin\",\r\n        \"Biscuit\",\r\n        \"hearth\"\r\n      ]\r\n    }\r\n  ]\r\n}","{\n  \"chapters\": [\n    {\n      \"n\": 41,\n      \"title\": \"Not Enough for Sam\",\n      \"text\": \"Deputy Sam Bramble arrived at the Rolling Pin Bakery just after closing, his hat in his hands and his boots dusted with sawdust from the village hall. Hattie set a mug of coffee and a warm apple turnover in front of him before he could say a word. Biscuit, sensing crumbs, settled hopefully on the stool beside him.\\n\\n\\\"Aunt Hattie,\\\" Sam said, \\\"Winnie tells me you have been reading gardening books again.\\\"\\n\\nHattie opened her little green notebook and turned it toward him. She showed him the pressed foxglove leaf, the envelope of crushed leaves, the empty hook in the potting shed, and the honey that never should have been in Lionel's cup. She spoke slowly and clearly, the way she used to explain long division to restless fourth graders.\\n\\nSam listened to every word, which she appreciated. Then he rubbed the back of his neck. \\\"It is a good story,\\\" he said gently. \\\"Maybe even the right one. But Detective Hask will not trade Delia for a pressed leaf and a hunch. A whiff of hand cream is not evidence. I need something real, something a judge can hold in his hands.\\\"\\n\\nHattie sighed and broke a turnover in half, giving the smaller piece to Biscuit. The cat ate it with great dignity. \\\"Then I will find you something real,\\\" she said. \\\"Something on paper, with ink on it.\\\" Sam smiled despite himself. \\\"That is exactly what worries me.\\\"\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"Deputy\",\n        \"turnover\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"notebook\",\n        \"foxglove\",\n        \"envelope\",\n        \"evidence\",\n        \"sawdust\",\n        \"coffee\",\n        \"judge\",\n        \"paper\",\n        \"Hattie\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 42,\n      \"title\": \"A Hand That Wasn't Delia's\",\n      \"text\": \"The next morning Hattie carried a basket of cinnamon buns across the square to the Sugar and Spoon. Delia Fairweather looked tired, but her shop smelled of butter and cardamom, and two customers were waiting at the counter. The village had kept its promise.\\n\\n\\\"I need to see your order book,\\\" Hattie said, once the customers had gone. \\\"The one for bake-off week.\\\" Delia pulled a fat ledger from under the register and flipped to a pink order form clipped inside. \\\"This came by mail the Friday before,\\\" she said. \\\"A plum cake for the judges' table, delivered to the tea tent at ten sharp. Signed by me, apparently, and approved by the committee.\\\"\\n\\nHattie bent close. The form was neat and businesslike, but the signature at the bottom was wrong. It leaned to the right in tidy, careful loops, the kind a person makes when they are copying something slowly. Delia's real handwriting was a quick, cheerful scrawl that looked as if a seagull had stepped in flour.\\n\\n\\\"I never signed this,\\\" Delia whispered. \\\"I thought the committee had chosen my cake because they liked it. I was so proud.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"Someone wanted your cake on that table at exactly that moment,\\\" Hattie said. She copied the loops into her green notebook, stroke by stroke, and tucked the pink form into an envelope. \\\"And someone practiced writing your name to make sure of it.\\\" Delia pressed a hand to her mouth. Outside, a gull laughed on the rooftop.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"signature\",\n        \"cinnamon\",\n        \"Delia\",\n        \"ledger\",\n        \"cardamom\",\n        \"register\",\n        \"customers\",\n        \"seagull\",\n        \"committee\",\n        \"flour\",\n        \"rooftop\",\n        \"butter\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 43,\n      \"title\": \"The Cake Was Clean\",\n      \"text\": \"Sam burst into the Rolling Pin at noon, so out of breath that Biscuit leapt off the windowsill in alarm. He waved a folded sheet of paper from the county lab.\\n\\n\\\"The results came back,\\\" he said. \\\"Aunt Hattie, you were right about the saucer. It held traces of foxglove, steeped strong, like a tea. But the plum cake was clean. Every crumb of it. Not a speck of anything but plums, sugar, and a great deal of butter.\\\"\\n\\nHattie sat down hard on the flour bin. She had believed it all along, but believing and knowing were two different things, like a list of ingredients and a finished pie.\\n\\nWithin the hour, Detective Lorna Hask had driven to the Sugar and Spoon herself. Hattie and Winnie watched from the bookshop window as the detective stood on the step, spoke a few brisk words, and shook Delia's hand. Delia burst into tears, and then, being Delia, she went inside and came back out with a box of lemon tarts for the detective to take home.\\n\\n\\\"She is officially cleared,\\\" Sam reported later. \\\"Hask says the poison was in the tea, not the cake. It was given to him before he ever sat down to judge.\\\" Winnie poured three cups of cocoa to celebrate, with extra whipped cream. Hattie smiled, but her mind was already moving. If the poison was in his morning tea, then the person who poisoned him had been close enough to brew it.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"results\",\n        \"foxglove\",\n        \"windowsill\",\n        \"crumb\",\n        \"detective\",\n        \"Lorna\",\n        \"lemon\",\n        \"tarts\",\n        \"cleared\",\n        \"poison\",\n        \"cocoa\",\n        \"Winnie\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 44,\n      \"title\": \"Closed on Tuesdays\",\n      \"text\": \"Oswald Finch was shelving large-print mysteries when Hattie came into the Pennywhistle Harbor library with a bag of oatmeal cookies. The librarian accepted them with the grave delight of a man who was not supposed to eat at the circulation desk.\\n\\n\\\"I have a strange question,\\\" Hattie said. \\\"On the morning of the bake-off, did you have many visitors?\\\" Oswald blinked at her over his half-moon spectacles. \\\"Visitors? Hattie, the library was closed. We are always closed on Tuesday mornings. That is when the furnace man comes, and when I take the book van out to the farms. I locked the doors at six the night before and did not open them again until two o'clock.\\\"\\n\\nHattie held very still. \\\"Nobody could have been reading in here?\\\" \\\"Not unless they came down the chimney,\\\" Oswald said, pointing at the brass sign by the door. Closed Tuesday Mornings, it said, in letters large enough for a seagull to read.\\n\\nHattie thanked him, tucked her notebook into her coat pocket, and walked slowly back along Harbor Street. The wind had turned cold, and the maple leaves skittered ahead of her like mice. Someone had sat across from her and sworn they had spent that whole morning quietly reading at the library. It had been a calm, simple alibi, and she had written it down without a second thought.\\n\\nNow it had crumbled like a dry pie crust. Hattie remembered exactly who had told her that.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"alibi\",\n        \"Oswald\",\n        \"librarian\",\n        \"cookies\",\n        \"spectacles\",\n        \"furnace\",\n        \"chimney\",\n        \"brass\",\n        \"maple\",\n        \"pocket\",\n        \"crust\",\n        \"oatmeal\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 45,\n      \"title\": \"An Invitation to Tea\",\n      \"text\": \"That evening Hattie sat at her kitchen table with a stack of cream-colored cards and her best fountain pen. Biscuit supervised from the top of the bread box, his tail twitching each time the pen scratched.\\n\\nWinnie came over with a thermos of cocoa and read over her shoulder. \\\"A thank-you tea,\\\" she said. \\\"For the five people who were questioned. Hattie Bramble, that is either very kind or very sneaky.\\\"\\n\\n\\\"It can be both,\\\" Hattie said. \\\"Everyone in this village has had a hard week. The least I can do is feed them.\\\" She wrote out each invitation by hand: Thursday at four, the Rolling Pin Bakery, scones and sandwiches and a pot of good strong tea. She addressed one to the Sugar and Spoon, one to the Gull's Rest Inn, one to the harbor office, one to the little cottage by the lighthouse, and one more to the inn for the guest who still had a room there.\\n\\nWinnie raised an eyebrow. \\\"And what will you serve?\\\" \\\"Lemon scones, cucumber sandwiches, and my maple shortbread,\\\" Hattie said. \\\"And two little pots on every table. One of honey. One of lavender sugar.\\\" Winnie set down her cocoa very carefully. \\\"Oh,\\\" she said. \\\"Oh, my.\\\"\\n\\nThe next morning Hattie delivered every card herself, smiling and chatting at each door. Then she went home, baked until midnight, and set out her very best china.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"fountain\",\n        \"thermos\",\n        \"sneaky\",\n        \"invitation\",\n        \"Thursday\",\n        \"scones\",\n        \"cucumber\",\n        \"shortbread\",\n        \"midnight\",\n        \"lighthouse\",\n        \"china\",\n        \"eyebrow\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 46,\n      \"title\": \"Honey and Lavender\",\n      \"text\": \"By four o'clock on Thursday, the Rolling Pin was warm and golden and smelled of browned butter. Five guests sat at the little round tables, looking as stiff as starched napkins. Winnie poured the tea. Biscuit wove between the chair legs, collecting pats and crumbs like taxes.\\n\\nHattie moved from table to table with a plate of maple shortbread, chatting about nothing much. The weather. The harbor. The new bench by the lighthouse. Then, as she set down a fresh pot of tea, she lifted the little jar of honey and smiled.\\n\\n\\\"Poor Lionel,\\\" she said lightly. \\\"Everyone knew he hated honey. Such a shame someone stirred it into his tea anyway. I suppose it hides a bitter taste beautifully.\\\" She turned the lavender sugar in the light. \\\"And lavender is so lovely, isn't it? Some people use it in hand cream. It clings to everything. Even a potting shed.\\\"\\n\\nThe room went very quiet. Someone cleared a throat. Someone else reached for a scone and thought better of it. Then, somewhere behind her, a teacup began to rattle against its saucer, a tiny, frantic sound, like a moth caught behind glass. It went on for a long moment before a hand steadied it.\\n\\nHattie did not turn around. She did not need to. She simply offered the nearest guest another piece of shortbread and kept her face as calm as a pond. But later, washing up, she noticed that one saucer had a fresh little chip on its rim.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"napkins\",\n        \"taxes\",\n        \"Biscuit\",\n        \"honey\",\n        \"lavender\",\n        \"bitter\",\n        \"rattle\",\n        \"saucer\",\n        \"moth\",\n        \"glass\",\n        \"pond\",\n        \"potting\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 47,\n      \"title\": \"The Photograph\",\n      \"text\": \"Winnie burst into the bakery the next morning before the bell had finished jingling. She was wearing her bathrobe under her raincoat and carrying a shoebox of photographs.\\n\\n\\\"I almost forgot,\\\" she said breathlessly. \\\"On bake-off morning I got up early to photograph the sunrise for the bookshop newsletter. I walked right past the inn. I only picked up the prints from the drugstore yesterday.\\\"\\n\\nShe spread them across the counter. Pink sky over the harbor. Gulls on the pilings. The white steeple of the church. And then one snapshot that made Hattie set down her rolling pin.\\n\\nIt showed the stone wall of the inn garden in the gray light before dawn. The little wooden gate stood open. Slipping through it, half turned away, was a figure in a long coat with a scarf pulled up high. The face was nothing but a pale blur, because Winnie had been shivering and the camera had wobbled. But the figure was small, and it was carrying something tucked tight under one arm, something that might have been a bundle of green stalks.\\n\\n\\\"The time is printed in the corner,\\\" Winnie said, tapping it. \\\"Six fourteen. Hours before the judging.\\\" Hattie studied the blurry figure for a long time. She noticed the small, neat shoes, dark with mud, and the careful way the coat was buttoned all the way to the top. \\\"Winnie Calloway,\\\" she said softly, \\\"you may have just taken the most important picture in Pennywhistle Harbor.\\\"\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"snapshot\",\n        \"bathrobe\",\n        \"raincoat\",\n        \"shoebox\",\n        \"sunrise\",\n        \"newsletter\",\n        \"pilings\",\n        \"steeple\",\n        \"figure\",\n        \"scarf\",\n        \"stalks\",\n        \"camera\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 48,\n      \"title\": \"The Green Notebook\",\n      \"text\": \"Long after midnight, Hattie sat at her kitchen table in her flannel nightgown with the little green notebook open in front of her. Biscuit slept in the fruit bowl, which he was not allowed to do, and she was too busy to scold him.\\n\\nShe tore out a fresh page for each clue and laid them out in rows, like cookies cooling on a rack. The cut foxglove in the walled garden. The empty hook where the shears should hang. The apron with its green stains. The honey in the cup of a man who hated honey. The travel kettle steaming in the Blue Suite at dawn. The train ticket from the day before anyone was expected. The recipe notebook that matched his famous book. The contract in his name alone. The letter firing someone close to him.\\n\\nThen the rest. Lavender in the shed. A muddy trowel. Small muddy boots. Crushed leaves in an envelope. The saucer, the timetable, the pressed leaf, the forged order, the alibi that had crumbled, and the small figure in Winnie's photograph.\\n\\nOne by one, Hattie drew lines between the pages with her pencil. Who had been close enough to brew his tea? Who had known the timetable? Who had the most to lose if that letter was sent?\\n\\nThe lines all met in the same place. Hattie sat back and let out a long breath. The kitchen clock ticked. Biscuit opened one eye. \\\"Yes,\\\" she told him quietly. \\\"I know. Now I only need to prove it.\\\"\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"flannel\",\n        \"nightgown\",\n        \"kettle\",\n        \"ticket\",\n        \"contract\",\n        \"trowel\",\n        \"timetable\",\n        \"pencil\",\n        \"clock\",\n        \"photograph\",\n        \"lavender\",\n        \"apron\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 49,\n      \"title\": \"The Same Hand\",\n      \"text\": \"At first light Hattie fetched Delia from the Sugar and Spoon and brought her back to the Rolling Pin, where she pulled an old tin box down from the top shelf. Inside were recipe cards from the Harvest Bake-Off, each one handwritten by a contestant and kept by the committee. Gordon had given her the box weeks ago, glad to be rid of it.\\n\\nBut Hattie did not want the old ones. She wanted the newest card, the one slipped into the tin this year by a guest who had offered a recipe for the inn's welcome dinner. Pear and ginger tart. Hattie had admired the tidy handwriting at the time.\\n\\nShe laid the card on the counter beside the pink order form. Delia leaned in. So did Biscuit, who had jumped up uninvited. The loops leaned the same way. The capital letters had the same careful curl, as if each one had been drawn slowly with the tongue between the teeth. The small d had the same odd little flag at the top. Even the ink, a deep navy blue, was the same.\\n\\n\\\"It is the same hand,\\\" Delia breathed. \\\"Hattie, whoever wrote this card forged my name.\\\"\\n\\nHattie nodded. She photographed both pages and called Sam, who came at once. He studied them for a long minute, and then he looked up at his aunt with something new in his eyes. \\\"That,\\\" he said, \\\"is something a judge can hold.\\\"\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"recipe\",\n        \"contestant\",\n        \"ginger\",\n        \"capital\",\n        \"tongue\",\n        \"navy\",\n        \"flag\",\n        \"counter\",\n        \"shelf\",\n        \"dinner\",\n        \"pear\",\n        \"Biscuit\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"n\": 50,\n      \"title\": \"Sunset in the Garden\",\n      \"text\": \"By evening, everyone had come. Sam had asked them politely, and Detective Lorna Hask had asked them less politely, and so the whole party gathered in the walled garden of the Gull's Rest Inn as the sunset spilled red and gold across the harbor.\\n\\nThe air smelled of woodsmoke and wet leaves. The last roses hung heavy on the wall, and the tall foxglove stalks still standing glowed purple in the long light. Dottie Quill stood by the potting shed with her arms folded. Oswald Finch had come, and Abe Tolliver too, his cap pulled low, because no one in Pennywhistle Harbor wanted to miss this. Winnie held Biscuit, who for once was not squirming.\\n\\nHattie stepped onto the flagstone path with her green notebook in one hand. She walked them through it slowly, the way she used to walk a classroom through a hard sum. The foxglove. The empty hook. The kettle at dawn. The honey he hated. The pressed leaf. The library that had been closed. The blurry figure at the gate. The handwriting that matched, loop for loop. Detective Hask said nothing, but she had stopped tapping her foot.\\n\\nThe sun touched the sea. A gull called once from the lighthouse. Five faces watched Hattie, and one of them had gone very pale. Hattie closed her notebook. She took a breath, lifted her chin, and raised her hand to point.\\n\\nIf you have gathered every clue, dear reader, turn now to the Final Reveal and find the name for yourself.\",\n      \"words\": [\n        \"woodsmoke\",\n        \"roses\",\n        \"purple\",\n        \"Dottie\",\n        \"Tolliver\",\n        \"flagstone\",\n        \"classroom\",\n        \"kettle\",\n        \"sunset\",\n        \"squirming\",\n        \"harbor\",\n        \"Hask\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"epilogue\": {\n    \"title\": \"Epilogue: Tea and Truth\",\n    \"text\": \"The name was Colette Marsh. For twelve years she had brewed Lionel's tea, typed his letters, and quietly invented the recipes that made him famous. Then he signed a contract for her newest cookbook under his own name alone and drafted a letter firing her. When she found it, something in her went cold.\\n\\nHer early train ticket showed she had arrived a day ahead of everyone, time enough to study the walled garden and cut the foxglove herself. On bake-off morning she steeped the leaves in her travel kettle, poured the tea into his monogrammed teacup, and stirred in honey to hide the bitterness. Weeks before, she had forged Delia's signature on a cake order, so that when Lionel faltered at the tasting table, everyone would blame the plum cake. Faced with the photograph and the matching handwriting, Colette did not argue. She folded her hands, and Detective Hask led her quietly away through the garden gate.\\n\\nDelia was cleared, and by Monday the Sugar and Spoon was busier than ever. Detective Hask came to the Rolling Pin with a sincere apology and bought two pies. Sam told everyone at the station that his aunt was the finest detective in Maine, and Biscuit was given a whole sardine on a china saucer.\\n\\nThe Harvest Bake-Off was held again the following Saturday. Hattie's apple pie won the blue ribbon, but she gave the prize money to Delia and put the kettle on for everyone.\"\n  }\n}"]};
